nderstanding the Material Better:**\n   - 60 students reported that they understood the material better after using the new method.\n   - 40 students reported that they did not understand the material better.\n   - This suggests that 60% of the students (60/100) reported an improvement in understanding, while 40% (40/100) did not.\n\n2. **Test Scores:**\n   - 70 students scored higher on the test after using the new method.\n   - 30 students scored lower on the test after using the new method.\n   - This suggests that 70% of the students (70/100) scored higher, while 30% (30/100) scored lower.\n\n3. **Interpreting the Results:**\n   - The survey indicates that the new teaching method led to a higher percentage of students scoring higher on the test (70%) compared to those who did not understand the material better (60%).\n   - However, the survey also shows that 40% of students did not understand the material better, which is a significant portion of the sample.\n\n4. **Evaluating the Statements:**\n   - **A) The new teaching method is effective in improving students' understanding of the material.**\n     - While 60% of students reported an improvement in understanding, this is not a majority (50% or more). Therefore, this statement is not strongly supported by the data.\n   - **B) The new teaching method is not effective in improving students' understanding of the material.**\n     - This statement is not supported by the data, as 60% of students reported an improvement.\n   - **C) The new teaching method is effective in improving students' test scores.**\n     - This statement is supported by the data, as 70% of students scored higher on the test after using the new method.\n   - **D) The new teaching method is not effective in improving students' test scores.**\n     - This statement is not supported by the data, as 70% of students scored higher on the test.\n\nGiven the analysis, the most likely true statement based on the provided data is:\n</think>\n<answer>C</answer>",
