messages:   40%|####      | 241/600 [16:25<02:20,  2.56it/s][["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nSmithtown University' s fund-raisers succeeded in getting donations from 80 percent of the potential donors they contacted. This success rate, exceptionally high for university fund-raisers, does not indicate that they were doing a good job. On the contrary, since the people most likely to donate are those who have donated in the past, good fund-raisers constantly try less-likely prospects in an effort to expand the donor base. The high success rate shows insufficient canvassing effort.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following, if true, provides more support for the argument?\n# Options:\nA) This year the average size of the donations to smithtown university from new donors when the university's fund-raisers had contacted was larger than the average size of donations from donors who had given to the university before.\nB) The majority of the donations that fund-raisers succeeded in getting for smithtown university this year were from donors who had never given to the university before.\nC) This year most of the donations that came to smithtown university from people who had previously donated to it were made without the university's fund-raisers having made any contact with the donors.\nD) Smithtown university's fund-raisers were successful in their contacts with potential donors who had never given before about as frequently as were fund-raisers for other universities in their contacts with such people.", "<think>.\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, we need to identify which option provides the most support for the argument that the high success rate of 80 percent does not indicate good job performance by Smithtown University's fund-raisers.The argument suggests that the high success rate is due to the fund-raisers focusing on less-likely prospects, rather than on those who are more likely to donate (i.e., past donors).\n\nLet's analyze each option:.\n\nA) This year the average size of the donations to smithtown university from new donors when the university's fund-raisers had contacted was larger than the average size of donations from donors who had given to the university before.\n- This option talks about the average donation size from new donors versus past donors.It does not directly address the issue of the high success rate being due to canvassing less-likely prospects.Therefore, it does not support the argument.\n\nB) The majority of the donations that fund-raisers succeeded in getting for smithtown university this year were from donors who had never given to the university before.\n- This option directly supports the argument.If the majority of the donations came from new donors (those who had never given before), it indicates that the fund-raisers were indeed focusing on less-likely prospects.This aligns with the argument that the high success rate is due to insufficient canvassing effort on past donors.Smithtown University's fund-raisers were not successful in their contacts with potential donors who had never given before as frequently as were fund-raisers for other universities in their contacts with such people.. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
