messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nIf you close all the wrong doors, truth will be shut out.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following is closest to the meaning of the above judgement?\n# Options:\nA) If truth is shut out, it must be that you close all the wrong doors.\nB) Truth can not be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed.\nC) If the truth is not shut out, then the door to all wrong must not be closed.\nD) To accept the truth, we must reject wrong.', '<think>.\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let\'s break down the given context and analyze each option step by step.\n\n### Context Analysis:.\nThe context states: "If you close all the wrong doors, truth will be shut out.".\n\nThis statement can be interpreted as a conditional statement: .\n- If P (closing all the wrong doors), then Q (truth will be shut out).\n\nIn logical terms, this can be written as:.\n\\[ P \\rightarrow Q \\].\n\n### Question Analysis:.\nWe need to find the option that is closest to the meaning of the given statement.\n\n### Option Analysis:.\n**A) If truth is shut out, it must be that you close all the wrong doors.**.\n- This is the contrapositive of the original statement.The contrapositive of \\( P \\rightarrow Q \\) is \\( \\neg Q \\rightarrow \\neg P \\).\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n- However, the original statement does not explicitly state that closing all wrong doors is necessary for truth to be shut out.It only states that if all wrong doors are closed, truth will be shut out.\n\n**B) Truth can not be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed.**.\n- This option suggests that truth cannot be shut out unless all wrong doors are closed.\n- This aligns with the original statement, as it implies that if truth is shut out, it must be because all wrong doors are closed.\n\n**C) If the truth is not shut out, then the door to all wrong must not be closed.**.\nTo reject the truth, we must accept wrong.\n- This statement implies that rejecting truth is dependent on accepting wrong.\n- This is the opposite of the original idea that accepting truth requires rejecting wrong.\n\n**A) Truth can be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed.**.\n- This option suggests that truth can be kept out if all wrong doors are closed.\n- This is a different concept from the original statement that truth can\'t be shut out unless all wrong doors are closed.\n\n### Conclusion:.\nThe option that is closest to the meaning of the given statement is:.\n\\[ \\text{B) Truth can not be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed.}\\].\n\nThis option correctly captures the conditional relationship implied by the original statement.\n</think>.\n<answer>\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
